  "summary": "Objective graphic design on mathematical grids: Akzidenz/Helvetica, flush-left ragged-right type, asymmetric balance.",
  "description": "Centered on Basel and Zürich designers (Müller-Brockmann, Ruder, Hofmann), the Swiss Style rebuilt graphic design around the grid, sans-serif neutrality, photography over illustration and clarity as ethics. It conquered corporate identity, wayfinding and textbook design worldwide and still underwrites minimal web and brand systems. Critics later charged it with cold sameness, birthing New Wave and grunge typography as counter-movements.",
